WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.030
The question is often phrased: why Mars?

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:18.786
Why send people across one hundred and forty million kilometers of empty sunshine, to a planet that has no breathable air, no liquid water, no magnetic field to shield them from cosmic rays, no fast way home?

00:00:18.786 --> 00:00:21.513
Mars is not where we are going next.

00:00:21.513 --> 00:00:24.240
Mars is what we are going next for .

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.891
Look at the screens in this orrery.

00:00:26.891 --> 00:00:38.859
Every rover, every orbiter, every lander placed on the Moon since 2007 — Chang'e, Chandrayaan, SLIM, Artemis flight planning — is, in some sense, a rehearsal.

00:00:38.859 --> 00:00:53.100
Every module of the International Space Station, every Tiangong segment, every long-duration crew that has lived continuously in microgravity for the past quarter-century — is a rehearsal.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:01:01.660
Every reusable booster lifting payload to orbit at a tenth of the cost it took twenty years ago — is a rehearsal.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:05.447
Mars is the destination that organizes the others.

00:01:05.447 --> 00:01:12.947
A Moon base teaches us to live off-world without going so far that a week's resupply is impossible.

00:01:12.947 --> 00:01:20.900
A continuous space station teaches us what a year of microgravity does to muscle, bone, vision, and mind.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:28.703
A rover landed by a robotic teleoperator teaches us autonomy at the distance where conversation cannot.

00:01:28.703 --> 00:01:33.096
The argument for Mars is not the argument that it is easy.

00:01:33.096 --> 00:01:33.853
It is not.

00:01:33.853 --> 00:01:38.853
The argument is that the questions Mars asks have no other answer.

00:01:38.853 --> 00:01:41.201
Is life common in the universe?

00:01:41.201 --> 00:01:45.443
Was Mars once Earth-like, and what did it lose, and how?

00:01:45.443 --> 00:01:48.246
Can a multi-celled biosphere migrate?

00:01:48.246 --> 00:01:49.685
Can a civilization?

00:01:49.685 --> 00:01:50.973
Look at this map.

00:01:50.973 --> 00:02:02.184
Look at the rovers already on it — silent, persistent, slowly tracing across the planet on errands set by teams two hundred million kilometers away.

00:02:02.184 --> 00:02:05.441
Every one of them is a postcard sent ahead.

00:02:05.441 --> 00:02:11.046
They are waiting for whoever comes to pick up the work where they left it.

00:02:11.046 --> 00:02:13.698
Mars is what we are going next for.

00:02:13.698 --> 00:02:16.728
The rest of this orrery is the syllabus.

